# rm434, the unmarked draughtsmen (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm434
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_lavinia_plankwick
written_by codex
title the unmarked draughtsmen
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=servant talk=chatty
traits temper=even talk=chatty warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk rehearses_a_halls_arrivals_with_plain_draughtsmen
likes spiced_cider draughts tidy_house summer_evenings
dislikes bad_manners wet_floors
fears a_guest_received_as_a_crest_instead_of_a_wolf
hopes a_house_that_can_welcome_an_unexpected_guest
topics work +0.6 pastimes +0.5 family +0.3 newcomer +0.3
styles comfort=seat gossip=discreet refuse=orderly tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# rehearses arrivals in a hall with plain draughtsmen and likes to know how a welcome works before adding family marks to a seating plan.

words
oath! = My missing draughtsman | Six wet thresholds
saying! = Seat the guest before the crest | Leave the door a square
piece = a plain draughtsman | the broad counter
board = a pocket board | my chequered cloth
corner = the corner square | a near corner
reserve = the spare piece | a pale spare
cider = a warm cider measure | the spiced cup
dryrag = a thick dry rag | the wiping cloth
stool = a low stool | the waiting seat
cross = a diagonal row | the slanting line
tray = a trial tray | the little salver
bellnote = the arrival bell | a light chime
place = the outside seat | a spare place
point = a corner nick | the plain edge
robe = the hanging cloth | the heavy curtain
salt = a pinch dish | the little salt bowl
ring = a cup mark | a pale ring
cuff = a folded cuff | the linen end
wax = a little seal | a thumb of wax
stripe = a checked strip | the chequered edge

any
opinion @1 houses mixed 0 : A House that wants to be known for courtesy must manage it even when the visitor has brought no flattering introduction.
opinion @1 church for 1 : The welcome I remember best from a quiet chapel belongs to a bench, a dry corner and nobody asking for credentials.
opinion @1 merchants mixed 0 : An explanation before the bill gives both buyer and seller a more agreeable evening.
opinion @1 road for 1 : It helps a host to remember the road the guest has just walked. A fine reception may begin simply with a place for wet boots.
opinion @1 newcomers for 2 : A new arrival may have no idea which doorway is intended. We in the hall ought to have considered that in advance.
opinion @1 festivals for 1 : A feast gives me scope to dispense a great deal of orderly pleasure and very little instruction once the guests are in.
opinion @1 dole for 1 : Setting out portions in the right order makes the last arrival no less deserving than the first.
opinion @1 city for 1 : An entire city has learnt to live in common on a hillside. That makes rearranging one hall seem a comfortably modest task.
opinion @1 watch mixed 0 : I would rather report the direction a visitor took than attach an imagined motive to the choice of doors.
opinion @1 times mixed 0 : Uncertain times are a sound reason to practise what can be made certain, especially where a tired arrival might sit.

grown
line @1 greet : Welcome. I can fit you in comfortably without upsetting the board. | Hello. We've ample room on the open side of this conversation.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : A new arrival, always rather interesting to a student of arrangements. | Welcome. We can attend to the particulars after you're comfortable.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. The pieces are all at their starting places. | An early arrival generally has an excellent choice of chairs.
line @1 greet.return : Yes, your greeting reached me. The spare piece has moved and can give you my ears. | Hello returned with proper attention now.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : A voice I can place without a seating chart. | You're the kind of arrival that simplifies arrangements.
line @3 greet.return [here.time=night] : Late welcome. Let's keep the light by the step for anybody following. | The night has left a quiet place between engagements.
line @1 again.open : You came by a slightly different square this time. | The board appears to have brought us together twice.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Come find the little fault in this procession. | Here. I've arranged a welcome that might amuse you.
line @1 farewell : May your next arrival find a competent host. | Walk into a clear and kindly doorway when you reach it.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll leave a place for another conversation. Goodbye. | Go with a little evening still to spare.
line @1 react.news : Let any conclusions wait outside till the account arrives. | You may occupy the whole quiet space while you tell it.
line @1 react.news.new : A new piece entirely. I shall consider the position. | That little board afforded me no advance notice.
line @1 react.news.known : Yes. My previous account puts the pieces in much the same order. | I heard the substance earlier, without that last small detail.
line @1 react.news.doubt : One piece has moved an improbable distance in that account. | I'd like to inspect the intermediate squares before accepting the end.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|crime|assault|loss|theft] : I'll find you somewhere quiet enough to let the news settle. | A loss doesn't need to arrange itself neatly before somebody offers a chair.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|work] : That does a great deal to brighten the arrangement of the day. | I'd quite gladly extend a summer evening to make room for news like that.
line @1 ask.how : Has your day left you a free square to rest on? | Have your own arrangements been reasonably kind to you?
line @1 answer.how : In fair order, with one small unexplained draughtsman occupying my thoughts. | Comfortably busy and extremely interested in the gaps.
line @1 decline : I'd have to surrender an earlier duty to give the matter its full attention. | I'd be arranging somebody else's time without the slightest right.
line @1 accept_decline : I shall adjust my own plans accordingly. Thank you. | The counter may remain precisely where you put it.
line @1 preclose : There's a small difficulty in my next arrangement that wants a second glance. | I'd like to set the board in order while I remember the sequence.
line @1 accept_preclose : Please attend to the next arrival in your own day. | You've made your intention clear and my arrangements will bear it.
line @1 thank : That was a thoughtful move, particularly from the end that couldn't see the doorway. | I noticed how neatly you made room for another. Thank you.
line @1 deflect_thanks : There was a spare position and I simply put it to work. | It took only the courtesy of leaving one useful square empty.
line @1 sympathise : You may have the outside chair if you'd like an easy departure later. | No need to speak for the whole time you sit with me.
line @1 reassure : Let's make space for one comfortable move before deciding the entire game. | A stiff arrangement can often be remedied by moving the least important stool.
line @1 ack : Yes. The next position follows quite sensibly. | I see which movement had escaped my eye.
line @1 give.advice : Try the arrival from the visitor's side of the door. | Always rehearse where the empty tray will return, as well as where the full one goes.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : A family occasion may dispense with the formal procession. | For kin, I'd gladly retire my practice board and make room by instinct.
line @4 greet.return [self.working=yes] : Hello. We are between two orderly arrivals just now. | I have only to shift this one piece and I am listening.
line @2 farewell.return [here.time=night] : May there be one sheltered square at your own door tonight. | Travel quietly where the household lights have gone down.
line @1 give.opinion : An distinguished guest and a shy arrival both deserve a clear way in. | I prefer courteous uncertainty to a splendidly certain mistake.
line @1 ask.detail : Where does the last arrival put a coat down? | Who has to step aside when the door swings inward?
line @1 answer.detail : The table on the near side takes the coat without a second crossing. | The visitor can step forward once the outer chair has shifted back.
line @1 react.self : A rather interesting problem for the quiet end of a room. | That would send my little board into useful rearrangement.
line @1 assess : A courteous course with very few unnecessary turns. | Well settled, provided the doorway actually opens that way.
line @1 tell.plan : I shall move the second chair before asking the guest to try the route. | I mean to leave a spare place reachable without a procession.
scene draughtarrival
when topic=pastimes self.likes=draughts
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I play an odd little game in which all the pieces are guests and nobody gets taken.
b : How do you win? | What do the dark and pale ones mean?
a : By getting a late guest to a comfortable place without making six earlier guests stand up. | Pale for folk still arriving and dark for folk settled. Their colours have nothing to do with their names.

scene cresttest
when topic=work self.job=servant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I make my first seating plan entirely without crests. | A curious thing happens when the host can't put noble names on the draughtsmen yet.
b : Won't the places all be wrong? | What curious thing?
a : A few may change. The fundamentals of proper light beside an open doorway needn't. | I attend to ordinary questions first. Is the chair dry, will the visitor see the welcome, can the plate reach the table.

scene lateentrance
when topic=work self.job=servant self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The late arrival gets a place reached without a long public walk past everybody already seated.
b : Wouldn't a grand entrance be better? | Why the outer end?
a : For a guest who wants one, perhaps. I don't consider lateness permission to provide a spectacle. | The guest can reach it promptly, and a warm dish can arrive before conversation turns to the lateness.

scene chairwheel
when topic=work self.job=servant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I rotate the whole board and look at the hall from the serving side. | The host's view of a fine arrangement is only one quarter-turn of the picture.
b : What changes when you turn it? | Do the guests move too?
a : I notice the tray hung up behind a high chair. That would reflect very poorly on the soup. | They don't need to. My own eyes need to approach from the door the working paws actually use.

scene learnpiece
when topic=work self.job=servant self.master=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : If I'm teaching, I give the learner the spare draughtsman before explaining its purpose. | A good lesson begins with an arrival the learner's plan did not anticipate.
b : What do you ask the learner to do? | A trick question, then?
a : Find it a civil place without upsetting the other pieces. Then we discuss which chair made the difference. | No trick. The hall hasn't promised to arrange its surprises in the order a lesson requires.

scene hotdrink
when topic=food self.likes=spiced_cider
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Cider with a little spice deserves a cup that can be lifted in one paw without the handle scorching it.
b : Do you check that personally? | How spicy a cup?
a : I try the temperature with an empty test cup beside the pour. I want comfort as well as warmth. | Enough to smell the spice while the apple still has something to say about the flavour.

scene quietgame
when topic=pastimes self.likes=draughts
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A proper game of draughts is quite restful after using the pieces to settle a hall.
b : More restful taking pieces? | What's different for you?
a : At least in a game nobody expects the captured counter to receive a pleasing supper afterwards. | A legal move is one the rules permit. In a hall an otherwise splendid move may inadvertently be rude.

scene tidycorner
when topic=home self.likes=tidy_house
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A very tidy room with no place to hang a wet coat is simply untidy a little ahead of schedule.
b : Which wall wants that peg? | Would you change the furniture for a coat?
a : Near enough to the door for the dripping part of the journey to be short. | Certainly. The guest and the coat are real. An idea of tidiness can afford to adjust.

scene wettest
when topic=weather here.weather=rain self.dislikes=wet_floors
does warn / react.self / assess
a : A wet floor changes the movement of every single piece on my imagined board.
b : All the chairs too? | How does the game change?
a : Particularly the chairs. I move one so nobody tries to step round it onto a slick patch. | The pleasantest route is the dry one till somebody's wiped up. Everything else can be made to follow.

scene poorwelcome
when topic=smalltalk self.dislikes=bad_manners
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Manners do not improve by being directed solely towards the best-dressed arrival. | I dislike courtesy that expires as soon as the crest is out of sight.
b : How would you detect that? | Would you mention it within an arriving guest's hearing?
a : Watch what happens to the wet cloak rather than to the person wearing it. A tired carrier matters too. | I'd fix the immediate discourtesy quietly. There would be time to speak plainly once the company had settled.

scene latebreeze
when topic=season self.likes=summer_evenings
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A long summer evening lets you move the visiting chairs entirely away from the lamps. | I enjoy making arrangements for guests while daylight gradually forgets to leave.
b : Does that save oil? | Why arrange them again?
a : Some. More importantly the company can see one another without leaning across a flame. | Because the hot part of the room moves away from the windows as the sun goes down. All very cooperative if I pay attention.

scene mildguest
when topic=newcomer
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A newcomer may follow the grandest doorway and end in entirely the wrong company. | I'd show the useful door before saying how easy the hall is to navigate.
b : Wouldn't a servant stop the mistake? | What if the new arrival can't remember the route?
a : Ideally, by being there early enough with the right direction that nobody needs stopping. | Then I would walk a little way. Company can sometimes do more than another beautifully accurate description.

scene parenthouse
when topic=family self.married=yes self.spouse
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I can put the draughtsmen away and spend an evening with #self.spouse# quite happily. | The first chair I'd leave free at my own table is for #self.spouse#.
b : What becomes of your plan then? | You'd put family before the game?
a : I am extraordinarily confident supper for two can proceed without a board and little counters. | With pleasure. A good plan is meant to serve the people who would like to sit down.

scene friendlyhabit
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd try an unlikely new arrangement out in conversation with #self.friend# first. | I can show #self.friend# the unfinished plan with three pieces still having nowhere sensible to sit.
b : Afraid of a critical eye? | Would a friend find the mistakes?
a : I rather hope for one. There is comfort in correcting a piece before an actual guest arrives. | A friend may ask why I'm proud of an entirely blocked door. That question can be useful.

scene greathall
when topic=home self.quarter=estate
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : An Estate hall has a public face and another made of all the little practical turnings behind it. | From my street at the Estate I sometimes see the arriving guests and the supper taking quite different routes.
b : Which face do you like better? | Do you watch both journeys?
a : The practical one. It's a particular pleasure to have everything work while the guests think it simply happened. | As often as possible. A fine warm arrival deserves something better than a cold dinner.

scene softdoor
when topic=work self.job=servant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A service door that closes with a bang becomes the loudest guest at every meal. | I listen to an empty door before settling the nearest chair.
b : You'd move the chair for a noise? | What can the empty door tell you?
a : Yes. Or see the door eased. Nobody deserves to be interrupted for the whole soup course by a latch. | Whether a quiet conversation can survive each passing tray. I'd give the company that chance.

scene tablesun
when topic=work self.job=servant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A polished table in the sun can leave one guest looking straight into a small sunrise. | I plan for the light as well as for the legs beneath a chair.
b : Wouldn't a bright room be pleasant? | How would you try the angle?
a : For most of the company. I should like to include the one directly facing the reflection in that pleasant prospect. | Sit where the guest is to sit. My board can suggest the question but actual eyes ought to give the answer.

scene replytime
when topic=gossip
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : Where a guest sat does not establish what passed between two seats. | I've seen a whole grand story inflated from the order in which chairs were occupied.
b : Wouldn't near neighbours speak? | What would you say about the visit?
a : Usually. I haven't any right to supply the actual conversation as well. | That the arrangements served their purpose. A good host needn't pay in confidences for a reputation for discretion.

scene hallprices
when topic=prices
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : An expensive chair which can't be moved by one working wolf is earning its decoration at an awkward rate. | I'd calculate a handsome article's cost in effort after we have paid in coin.
b : Would you choose a cheap chair? | Is beauty a waste of money?
a : A sound one with an intelligent back and four reliable feet. I can fit a little beauty round that. | Not at all. Beauty that takes three tired wolves to lift had better give somebody extraordinary pleasure.

scene hardless
when topic=times world.times=lean|hard
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A smaller supper doesn't require smaller manners. | I can plan a courteous table with fewer dishes if times demand it.
b : Which expense goes first? | Could anybody tell the difference?
a : The irrelevant procession of lids. Keep the food warm and let the actual dish speak honestly. | Of course. I wouldn't deceive guests about the quantity. I'd see they all got an equally considered share.

scene raincoat
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A rain-soaked arrival takes up the space of one wolf and three sensible precautions. | What's on the board for a soaking visitor?
b : A dripping cloak makes more than one piece? | Do you want me to guess?
a : It earns one piece for the peg, one for the drying cloth and one for the dry seat after the coat comes off. | I'll spare you the counting. First the cloak goes somewhere drip-safe, then we offer a cloth and a chair.

scene darkpaths
when topic=day here.time=night
does warn / react.self / assess
a : At night an unused dark square can be the most important bit of the route. | Don't leave a stool in a path made narrow by shadows.
b : There's plenty of room in daylight. | Would one candle solve it?
a : Yes. Darkness makes a different room without moving any of the walls. | If it shows the obstacle. I'd prefer to move the stool first, then spend the candle on a more useful sight.

scene lefthand
when topic=smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I set out the game for an imaginary guest who arrives with one paw full. | One free paw is a fairly severe test of a polite arrangement.
b : An important parcel? | What does one free paw need?
a : Any damp errand bundle will do. I simply want to know whether the guest can manage the door gracefully. | A straightforward latch and somewhere nearby to put the bundle. Two modest courtesies before any formal greeting.

scene churchquiet
when topic=faith
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : When I pray I sometimes picture an empty square just inside the door. | I wonder who has found a room too grand or too busy to enter.
b : An empty place for whom? | Do you mean the chapel door?
a : For a visitor I haven't correctly imagined. A prayer that specifies every single guest leaves very little room for a surprise. | That door certainly. I should also like to remember the lesson at ordinary doors during the ordinary week.

scene privatehistory
when topic=memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Some expressions seen across a big room belong in no account of the event. | I can remember an evening by the placing of the chairs without needing to catalogue the private faces in them.
b : Do you remember the faces too? | Is that deliberate?
a : Often. Seeing a thing does not confer the right to discuss it afterwards. | Much of the time. I'd like my own difficult moments to be treated with comparable restraint.

scene teachwait
when topic=work self.master=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A learner has thought of a first answer. I like to leave time for the more interesting second one. | Sometimes the correct thing to teach is a useful pause.
b : What's special about the second answer? | Wouldn't the work go quicker if you said?
a : It often accounts for the wet cloak forgotten in the first. That's progress anybody can see. | Today, perhaps. The next crowded evening deserves someone who can see the cloak without my pointing.

scene tactlearn
when topic=pastimes self.master=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : An unmarked draughtsman makes an excellent teaching guest because it cannot be embarrassed by an awkward first trial. | I'd rather make a mistake on the board than over somebody else's hot dinner.
b : Can it object to a bad seat? | Who decides the trial succeeded?
a : The learner tries sitting there, which supplies the objection with considerably more life than the piece could manage. | We can walk the path with an empty tray. If that goes through without nudging a sleeve, the plan has promise.

scene latewagon
when topic=caravan world.wagons=late
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A delayed wagon calls for a different supper plan, not an announcement that supper has ceased to exist. | I keep a second arrangement for the evening's late goods.
b : What would you set out? | Isn't that twice the work?
a : What the cupboard actually contains. It is a particularly obliging ingredient, always present under a lifted lid. | Only on the board. The alternative to a little extra planning can be an entire hall of hungry questions.

scene receivedload
when topic=caravan world.wagons=in
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Goods newly come into town still need a clear landing place behind the kitchen. | A delivery that finds the service entrance full of chairs can give the evening a remarkable start.
b : Who's using a doorway for chairs? | What sort of start?
a : Nobody if my rehearsal was worth the quarter hour. I like to identify the possibility before it acquires weight. | The kind with everybody talking at once and nobody carrying anything inward. I prefer to avoid it.

scene planahead
when topic=dreams
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd like a household where any worker can describe why the entrance remains clear, not just repeat an order to keep it so. | My ideal hall would welcome a stranger quite intelligently in my absence.
b : You'd make your own position unnecessary? | What would you have taught everybody?
a : For a quiet afternoon, certainly. I should take the opportunity to sit with my cider. | To see the arrival before the inconvenience and the wet floor before the hurried feet. The rest can be arranged.

scene gueststorm
when topic=weather here.weather=storm
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A loud storm brings the chairs closer without anybody having to issue a formal invitation. | I enjoy seeing what a room chooses to do for its company when the thunder interrupts.
b : What does it generally choose? | Would you still interfere?
a : The seats nearer the light fill first. I see whether the quietest guest has retained a good place. | Only with some practical need. A room full of sensible wolves doesn't need directing every time the sky speaks.

story firstdrape [topic=work|memories self.job=servant]
  : I planned two elegant files on my board and walked my splendid route through the real hall, empty dish aloft.
  : My sleeve caught the curtain as I rounded the first corner. No piece on the board had been issued a sleeve.
  b : Did you add some cloth to the counters?
  : I added the curtain to the plan. Dressing an entire set of draughtsmen looked an unreasonable use of a fine afternoon.

story blackpiece [topic=pastimes|memories]
  : I held a reception on half my draughts board and played a real game on the other half. The two occasions stayed separate for almost an hour.
  : An distinguished guest took two jumps and became an entirely accidental champion.
  b : Did the guest return to the table?
  : Gave the honourable champion a comfortable chair and started playing the actual game from its proper first position.

story saucersquare [topic=food|memories]
  : I left my cider standing on the cloth board while I moved some chairs. Came back to find one square handsomely marked with a round wet seal.
  : It became the wet floor in every subsequent rehearsal until the cloth was washed.
  b : What did the mark teach you?
  : To attend to spills and to stop inviting the cider into a discussion intended for dry wooden counters.

story latebird [topic=memories|pastimes]
  : I set a game out on a low sill one summer evening. A bird hopped onto a corner and looked hard at the only unoccupied square.
  : I felt it my duty as a host to make a little room.
  b : Did the bird stay?
  : No. It took the offered square as its point of departure. A clear exit is a form of hospitality too.

story welcomefoot [topic=memories|travel]
  : I left by the service door and returned by the guest entrance to see what a visitor would see. There was my best umbrella leaning against a chair.
  : It had gone missing two mornings earlier.
  b : And the welcome?
  : Quite improved once the umbrella went home. An exercise in observation with a particularly practical reward.

rule @door
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Leave that first square unoccupied. The door wants it. | A well-set chair and a free turn for the next arrival.

rule @work
when concept=bark working=yes self.job=servant
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Check the returning tray's route as well as the full tray's. | Place the drying cloth where the guest can reach it without asking.

rule @lesson
when concept=bark self.master=yes
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Now try that opening move from the visitor's end of the board. | One new guest at the door. See which of your pieces must shift.

rule @rain
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Wet cloak, dry seat, a clear patch between. | A hurried boot will find any wet square we leave.

rule @summertime
when concept=bark self.likes=summer_evenings
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Pull the seats round to the long light while we have it. | Time enough for cider and another move before the lamps.

rule @tact
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 8h
say The real guest doesn't need to know we're still moving chairs. | Quiet feet round the outside of a settled conversation, please.

dlg @1 resident.hub : I have my guests safely arranged in wood for the moment. A living question will have my full attention.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#, you'll recognise the layout, though the extra visitor has changed the interesting part.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : We've a quiet hour well suited to considering the day's little surprises.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : Our present distance allows an entirely civil conversation if we both choose to use it.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=servant] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. My concern begins a few steps before the visitor enters and lasts till the departing feet are safely out of the weather.
dlg @2 resident.job : When my preparations are good, the next wolf has leisure to enjoy the occasion.
dlg @1 resident.bye : May your next welcome feel entirely natural, however much trouble went into planning it.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=no] : No tale from me. My little wooden guests are a credit to themselves on the matter of discretion.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : Word reached me concerning #topic.subject#. The account is that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. A report only, not a matter I've settled by arranging a few chairs.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : We have a report carried from #world.news_from#. The substance is this. #world.news#.
dlg @1 master.learn [here.teaches=yes] : #here.trade_title.capitalize#. I can demonstrate an opening arrangement and explain the work that keeps it useful. Which arrangement interests you?
dlg @2 master.learn [here.teaches=no] : I've already given the learner's place to somebody, but there's still room for an interested question.
dlg @1 master.work : The pleasing part is receiving people. The patient part is getting very ordinary arrangements right a hundred times in advance.
dlg @1 master.wages : You won't receive wages simply for learning. You'll practise beside me, learn more quickly, and the value of your own completed work is yours.
dlg @1 master.takes [here.take_on=yes] : I have room to guide a single pupil. I'd be very comfortable taking you. Ask plainly and we can settle the arrangement.
dlg @2 master.takes [here.take_on=no] : I need time and trust for one learner. At present there's an obstacle. #here.take_on_why#.
dlg @1 master.taken : We've agreed then. I'll put an extra stool where you can observe both the guests and the service door.
dlg @1 master.refused : The place can't be settled that way just now. #topic.why#.
dlg @1 master.bye : Keep an interested eye on the next doorway you pass.
graft resident.hub
choice Would you mind showing me the arrangement on the board? -> guestboard
node guestboard
say Start with this plain piece. It arrives late, carrying a wet cloak and hoping to avoid a parade past the rest of the company. Which gap would you leave open?
free
choice I'd try the entrance at the near corner. -> nearestmove
choice I should like to discuss something else. -> hub
node nearestmove
say Quite sensible. Put the chair near enough to offer, far enough back to let the door swing. Now the guest can choose whether to settle or go further in.
choice I'll keep that square clear. -> hub
choice I ought to see to my own arrangements. -> bye

graft master.learn
choice May I try a simple exercise with the draughtsmen? -> firstboard
node firstboard
